---
import StandardPageLayout from "../../layouts/StandardPageLayout.astro";
import FinancialStatementKey from "../../components/FinancialStatementKey.astro";
import Glossary from "../../components/Glossary.astro";
import PageToc from "../../components/PageToc.astro";
import "../../styles/standards-poster.css";
import "../../styles/extended.css";
import HistoryTimeline from "../../components/HistoryTimeline.astro";
import history from "../../data/history.json";
import metadata from "../../data/standards/ukgaap/sorps.json";
---

<StandardPageLayout
  code={metadata.code}
  standardType={metadata.framework}
  title={metadata.title}
  description={metadata.description}
  issued={metadata.issued}
  latestAmendment={metadata.latestAmendment}
  status={metadata.status}
  summary={metadata.summary}
  bannerNotice={metadata.bannerNotice}
>
  <Fragment slot="toc">
    <PageToc
      items={[
        { id: "s1", label: "Conceptual Framework Linkage" },
        { id: "s2", label: "Scope – SORP-Making Bodies and Sectors Covered" },
        { id: "s3", label: "Status – \"Comply or Explain,\" Not a Separate GAAP" },
        { id: "s4", label: "The Charities SORP – the Statement of Financial Activities (SoFA)" },
        { id: "s5", label: "Fund Accounting – Restricted, Unrestricted, Designated, and Endowment Funds" },
        { id: "s6", label: "Other Prominent SORPs" },
        { id: "s7", label: "Worked Example – the Charities SORP's SoFA in Outline" },
        { id: "s8", label: "Worked Example – Classifying a Donation Into the Correct Fund" },
        { id: "s9", label: "Disclosure Requirements" },
        { id: "s10", label: "Critical Judgments" },
        { id: "s11", label: "Relationship to IAS/IFRS and to US GAAP's ASC 958" },
        { id: "s12", label: "Statement Mapping" },
        { id: "s13", label: "Related Standards" },
        { id: "s14", label: "Glossary" },
      ]}
    />
  </Fragment>
  <div class="wrap">

    <div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Sector-specific application guidance, not a competing accounting framework</div><div class="principle">A Statement of Recommended Practice (SORP) provides SECTOR-SPECIFIC guidance on how to APPLY FRS 102 (or, occasionally, other underlying standards) within a PARTICULAR industry – charities, higher/further education, registered social housing providers, limited liability partnerships (LLPs), retirement benefit (pension) schemes, and investment/authorised funds each have their OWN SORP. A SORP does NOT establish a SEPARATE accounting framework – it sits WITHIN, and supplements, the "new UK GAAP" hierarchy (FRS 100–105), addressing genuinely SECTOR-SPECIFIC transactions and presentation needs that the general standards don't fully anticipate.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope – SORP-Making Bodies and Sectors Covered</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Recognised bodies developing sector guidance under FRC oversight</div><div class="principle">SORPs are developed by SORP-MAKING BODIES formally recognised by the Financial Reporting Council (FRC) – sector representative organisations with the expertise to translate general FRS 102 principles into PRACTICAL, industry-specific guidance. The MOST prominent SORPs cover CHARITIES, FURTHER AND HIGHER EDUCATION institutions, REGISTERED SOCIAL HOUSING providers, LLPs, RETIREMENT BENEFIT schemes, and INVESTMENT/AUTHORISED FUNDS.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>Status – "Comply or Explain," Not a Separate GAAP</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">A recommended practice with real practical force, though not itself an FRS</div><div class="principle">A SORP is NOT itself a Financial Reporting Standard with the SAME binding status as FRS 102 – but DEPARTURE from a relevant SORP's RECOMMENDATIONS must be JUSTIFIED and DISCLOSED, giving SORPs a genuine "COMPLY OR EXPLAIN"-STYLE practical force within their sector. In PRACTICE, entities within a covered sector almost UNIVERSALLY follow the applicable SORP, given the STRONG expectation of compliance and the SORP's role in demonstrating genuine sector-appropriate "true and fair" presentation.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>The Charities SORP – the Statement of Financial Activities (SoFA)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">A distinctive combined statement, unique to charity-sector reporting</div><div class="principle">The most PROMINENT SORP – the CHARITIES SORP (FRS 102) – requires charities to present a "STATEMENT OF FINANCIAL ACTIVITIES" (SoFA) IN PLACE of a conventional Profit and Loss Account – a DISTINCTIVE statement COMBINING all incoming resources (donations, grants, trading income, investment income) and resources expended (charitable activities, raising funds, governance costs), analysed by FUND type (Section 5), arriving at a NET movement in funds for the period – reflecting the fundamentally DIFFERENT nature of charitable, non-reciprocal income compared to ordinary commercial revenue.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>Fund Accounting – Restricted, Unrestricted, Designated, and Endowment Funds</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">A UK charity-sector parallel to US GAAP's donor-restriction net asset classification</div>
      <div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2fr;"><span>Fund type</span><span>Description</span></div>
      <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2fr;"><span>Unrestricted funds</span><span>Funds the trustees can use FREELY for any of the charity's purposes</span></div>
      <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2fr;"><span>Restricted funds</span><span>Funds a DONOR has stipulated must be used for a SPECIFIC purpose</span></div>
      <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2fr;"><span>Designated funds</span><span>UNRESTRICTED funds the TRUSTEES (not a donor) have earmarked for a specific future purpose – the charity's own internal, reversible designation</span></div>
      <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2fr;"><span>Endowment funds</span><span>Funds a donor requires to be held as CAPITAL (PERMANENT endowment, retained indefinitely) or for a SPECIFIED period (EXPENDABLE endowment), rather than spent immediately</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="note">This fund STRUCTURE echoes – but is not identical in terminology to – US GAAP's ASC 958 "net assets WITH donor restrictions" vs "net assets WITHOUT donor restrictions" classification, and its board-designated "quasi-endowment" concept (see the companion ASC 958 poster).</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>Other Prominent SORPs</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Higher/further education, social housing, LLPs, and pension schemes</div><div class="principle">The FURTHER AND HIGHER EDUCATION SORP addresses university and college-specific transactions (research grants, tuition fee recognition, specific fund accounting). The REGISTERED SOCIAL HOUSING PROVIDERS SORP addresses housing association-specific matters (social housing property costing, capital grants). The LLPs SORP addresses partnership-specific equity and members' capital presentation. The PENSIONS SORP addresses retirement benefit SCHEME (not sponsoring employer) financial statements – broadly analogous in PURPOSE to the US GAAP ASC 960/962/965 plan accounting Topics, presenting a scheme's OWN net assets and actuarial liability information.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>Worked Example – the Charities SORP's SoFA in Outline</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Combining all resource flows into a single statement</div>
      <div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;"><div class="wk-title">Example: A charity's simplified SoFA</div><div class="wk-body">A charity receives £400,000 of unrestricted donations, £150,000 of restricted grant income for a specific project, and incurs £480,000 of charitable activity costs and £60,000 of governance costs during the year.</div><div class="wk-answer">The SoFA presents TOTAL incoming resources of £550,000 (£400,000 unrestricted + £150,000 restricted), LESS total resources expended of £540,000 (£480,000 + £60,000), arriving at a <strong>net movement in funds of £10,000</strong> for the year – with the movement further ANALYSED between the unrestricted and restricted fund COLUMNS, showing how each fund type individually moved.</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>Worked Example – Classifying a Donation Into the Correct Fund</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Donor stipulation drives fund classification</div>
      <div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;"><div class="wk-title">Example: A donor-specified building appeal</div><div class="wk-body">A charity receives a £50,000 donation, with the donor's letter explicitly stating the funds must be used ONLY toward the charity's ongoing building renovation appeal.</div><div class="wk-answer">Since the DONOR has stipulated a specific PURPOSE, the £50,000 is classified as a <strong>RESTRICTED FUND</strong> – it cannot be used for the charity's general purposes until (and unless) applied toward the building renovation, distinct from how the trustees' OWN internal earmarking of unrestricted funds (a designated fund) would be treated.</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Disclosure Requirements</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">What relevant SORPs typically require</div><div class="check-list"><span>Analysis of income and expenditure (or activities) by fund type</span><span>Nature and purpose of each material restricted or designated fund</span><span>Sector-specific disclosures (e.g. trustees' report content for charities)</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>Critical Judgments</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cn span2"><div class="ctitle cn">Where judgement matters most</div>
      <div class="two-col"><div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGEMENTS</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Classifying resources correctly between restricted, unrestricted, designated, and endowment funds based on donor stipulations</span><span>Determining whether a departure from SORP guidance is justified in the specific circumstances</span></div></div>
      <div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT ESTIMATES</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Allocation of shared costs (e.g. governance, support costs) across activities and funds</span></div></div></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>Relationship to IAS/IFRS and to US GAAP's ASC 958</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Comparison across frameworks</div>
      <div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr 1fr;"><span>Feature</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-warning);">UK SORPs (e.g. Charities SORP)</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-info);">IFRS / US GAAP</span></div>
      <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr 1fr;"><span>Dedicated not-for-profit standard</span><span>YES – the Charities SORP built on FRS 102</span><span>IFRS: NO dedicated standard. US GAAP: YES – ASC 958</span></div>
      <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr 1fr;"><span>Fund/net asset classification</span><span>Restricted / unrestricted / designated / endowment funds</span><span>ASC 958: net assets with/without donor restrictions (simplified from a former three-tier system)</span></div>
      <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr 1fr;"><span>Combined activities statement</span><span>Statement of Financial Activities (SoFA)</span><span>ASC 958 uses a Statement of Activities, conceptually similar but not identically structured</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="note">IFRS itself has NO dedicated not-for-profit contribution accounting standard (see the ASC 958 poster's discussion of this gap) – the UK's Charities SORP, built on FRS 102, fills an analogous role to ASC 958 within the UK/Irish reporting landscape, even though the two frameworks' specific fund/net-asset terminology and statement structures differ.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>Statement Mapping</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Where SORP-driven presentation lands</div>
      <div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Event</span><span>Statement(s) affected</span><span>Notes</span></div>
      <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Charity income/expenditure</span><span>Statement of Financial Activities</span><span>Replaces the conventional Profit and Loss Account for charities</span></div>
      <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Restricted/designated fund movements</span><span>Fund-analysed columns within the SoFA</span><span>Tracked separately by fund type</span></div>
      </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>Related Standards</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Cross-reference map</div>
      <div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>Standard</span><span>Connection</span></div>
      <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>FRS 102</span><span>Supplies the underlying general accounting framework SORPs apply and supplement within each sector</span></div>
      <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>FRS 100</span><span>References SORPs within the broader UK/Irish financial reporting framework hierarchy</span></div>
      </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s14"><span class="num">14</span><h2>Quick-Reference Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cn span2">
      <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Statement of Financial Activities (SoFA)</div><div class="glossary-def">The combined income, expenditure, and fund movement statement required by the Charities SORP in place of a conventional Profit and Loss Account.</div></div>
      <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Restricted fund</div><div class="glossary-def">Charitable funds a donor has stipulated must be used for a specific purpose.</div></div>
      <div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;"><div class="glossary-term">Designated fund</div><div class="glossary-def">Unrestricted funds a charity's own trustees have earmarked for a specific future purpose, remaining reversible and within the charity's own discretion.</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <FinancialStatementKey />

  </div>
  <section id="history">
    <HistoryTimeline items={history.sorps || []} />
  </section>
</StandardPageLayout>
